========================================
ABSOLUTE POSITIONING WITH top: 10px
========================================

In this example, .box2 has:

    position: absolute;
    top: 10px;

When position: absolute is applied, .box2 is
removed from the normal document flow.
It no longer occupies space in the layout.

Because of this, .box3 moves upward and takes
the original space of .box2.

Since no ancestor element has a positioned
value (relative, absolute, fixed, or sticky),
.box2 uses the initial containing block
(viewport) as its reference.

The property:

    top: 10px;

positions .box2 10 pixels from the top of
the viewport, not from its original place
inside the div.

As a result:
- .box2 shifts toward the top of the page
- .box3 remains in normal flow
- Overlapping occurs

This behavior is expected when using
position: absolute with offset values.
